        "floor_dbfs_per_bin": -20.3268793791391,
        "pilot": {
          "present": false,
          "peak_over_local_median_db": 0.22,
          "measurement": "FM discriminator over all 45 s, Welch 32768-point (12.21 Hz resolution): no 19 kHz line, so this is not a stereo FM broadcast"
        },
        "not_an_image": {
          "mirror_center_hz": 101134660.7778529,
          "mirror_snr_db": -6.0756299280143296,
          "mirror_presence_on_bins": 0,
          "note": "the IQ mirror of this frequency about the tuner centre is empty, so it is not the image of a stronger signal; nor is it a 10 MHz reference harmonic (those land on multiples of 10 MHz, 334.7 kHz away)"
        },
        "presence": {
          "on_bins": 1646,
          "total_bins": 1648,
          "bin_s": 0.027306666666666667,
          "level_db_min": 5.186153620316036,
          "level_db_median": 7.886348057710698,
          "level_db_max": 8.435407991715264,
          "note": "continuous and very steady: 50 ms block power varies by under 0.5 dB over 20 s"
        },
        "note": "No one anticipated this one: the capture README does not mention it, and T-289 could only record what it is not. T-317 identified it from the other captures at this site: it is harmonic 43 of a free-running ~2.3364 MHz oscillator whose harmonics 44 and 45 are visible at 102.78 and 105.12 MHz in the 2026-09-13 wideband recordings, outside this capture's 2.4 MHz span - which is why one 2.4 MHz window could not identify it. It is a real, continuous, ~28 kHz emission inside the analogue passband, and the pipeline found it blind and confirmed it (emitter at 100.46054 MHz, 45 detections, SNR peak 13.1-14.5 dB), but it carries no information: its width is the fundamental's frequency noise multiplied by 43. It stays role=emission rather than role=artefact because it is energy the receiver actually took in, not a spur the tuner synthesised, and nothing here shows it to be internal; see identification.open.",
        "t_start_s": 0.0,
        "duration_s": 45.00138666666667,
        "modulation_measurement": "No information-bearing modulation could be measured, and the 28 kHz width is not modulation. Constant modulus: |z| kurtosis 1.386 in a +-20 kHz channel against 2.03 for the noise-only controls at 100.150 and 100.650 MHz, i.e. Rice-distributed as a constant-envelope carrier in noise, not Rayleigh. No discrete carrier: at 1.14 Hz RBW (20 x 0.874 s blocks) there is no line anywhere under the hump - the carrier is smeared across the whole 28 kHz by its own frequency noise. No symbol structure: x^2 and x^4 spectra peak at only 3.2 and 4.8 dB over their local median (no recoverable carrier or symbol rate), the instantaneous-frequency histogram is unimodal (not FSK), and the discriminator spectrum carries no line that the capture chain does not explain (see capture_artefact in hackriff:provenance). No programme content: the discriminator's 0.1-1 kHz power sits 6.66 dB over its own 8-15 kHz noise reference with a standard deviation of 0.30 dB across 45 one-second blocks (1-3 kHz: 1.11 dB, sd 0.18 dB). Speech or music varies second to second; this does not vary at all. What the discriminator does show is a 1/f-shaped, stationary low-frequency process - oscillator frequency noise.",
        "identification": {
          "verdict": "the 43rd harmonic of a free-running ~2.3364 MHz oscillator: a local unintentional emitter (EMI), not a transmitter",
          "confidence": "high for the harmonic family; the emitter itself is unidentified and radiated-vs-conducted is untested",
          "task": "T-317 (2026-09-16)",
          "fundamental_hz": 2336387.0,
          "harmonic_n": 43,
          "fundamental_note": "f0 = this capture's measured centre / 43. The fundamental itself is at 2.34 MHz, far outside every capture, and was never observed directly.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "id": "siblings",
              "text": "Two more members of the family are visible in the 2026-09-13 wideband captures (98 MHz centre, 20 Msps, which span 90.6-105.4 MHz and so reach where this 2.4 MHz recording cannot). With the amp off they sit at 100.446474, 102.782818 and 105.118406 MHz; dividing each by 43, 44 and 45 gives f0 = 2.335965, 2.335973 and 2.335965 MHz - three independent estimates agreeing to 9 Hz (3.7 ppm). Fitting f = n*f0 + b over the three gives b = +67 Hz against an f0 of 2.336 MHz, so the harmonic indices are pinned exactly: an index off by one would put b at +-2.34 MHz. The amp-on capture of the same session gives the same picture one oscillator-drift step away (f0 = 2.335413, 2.335390, 2.335402 MHz; b = +10.9 kHz)."
            },
            {
              "id": "width_scales_with_n",
              "text": "Each member's rms spectral width divided by its harmonic number is the same: 138, 120 and 137 Hz (amp off) and 136, 134 and 130 Hz (amp on). That is what multiplying one jittery fundamental by n does - the fundamental carries about 134 Hz rms of frequency noise and harmonic 43 carries 43 times it, 5.8 kHz. Three unrelated emitters cannot produce widths proportional to a harmonic index. Their normalised line shapes also correlate at 0.967-0.988."
            },
            {
              "id": "not_a_broadcast_shape",
              "text": "In the same 20 Msps survey the real WFM stations measure 120-126 kHz of 99 % occupied bandwidth (101.2993 and 104.4946 MHz) against 23-28 kHz for every member of this family, and they sit on the US 200 kHz odd-tenth raster to within a few kHz while the family members sit 18-83 kHz off it."
            },
