            {
              "hypothesis": "mono FM broadcast or any programme feed",
              "how": "no RDS, and the discriminator's audio-band power is constant to 0.30 dB standard deviation over 45 one-second blocks; 34.7 kHz off the nearest US channel (100.5 MHz) while the two real stations here are within 1.34 kHz of theirs"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "receiver IQ image",
              "how": "the mirror about the tuner centre, 101.134661 MHz, is empty (SNR -6.1 dB, 0 of 1648 bins present) - measured by T-289"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "10 MHz reference harmonic",
              "how": "the reference grid is multiples of 10 MHz; the nearest is 334.7 kHz away - measured by T-289"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "DC / LO leakage",
              "how": "334.7 kHz from the tuned centre, and the DC line is separately annotated 585.9 Hz wide at 100.800000 MHz"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "receiver intermodulation between the two WFM stations",
              "how": "an IM product of two ~128 kHz-wide WFM carriers is at least as wide as its parents, and this is 28 kHz. The IM3 partners are also absent: the 2f2-f1 partner at 100.882 MHz measures +0.06 dB of mean excess (4.4 dB peak, noise) and the 2f1-f2 partner would be at 102.132 MHz, outside the recorded span. Overload was measured false (0 of 20,000,000 ci8 components at the rail)"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "the receiver's 8 kHz spur comb",
              "how": "this capture carries a wideband line comb spaced 7999.9 Hz, at tuner DC - 674 Hz + n*8 kHz, 12-15 dB over the floor at 1.14 Hz RBW, right across the 2.4 MHz span (it is what a USB 2.0 microframe rate looks like). The emission is not on that grid - it would need n = 41.75 - and it is a 28 kHz hump, not a line. Two comb lines do cross the hump, at 100.455325 and 100.479324 MHz, and are the comb, not the emission"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "the 209.43 kHz spur comb of the wideband captures",
              "how": "the 20 Msps captures carry a second comb spaced 209.43 kHz across 90-105 MHz; the family is not on it either ((100.446 - 91.750)/0.20943 = 41.52)"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "an SCA / subcarrier service of a nearby station",
              "how": "an SCA rides inside its host's +-100 kHz baseband; this is 833 kHz from 101.2987 MHz and 765 kHz from 99.6999 MHz, far outside both"
            },
            {
              "hypothesis": "a data, telemetry or digital-voice link",
              "how": "no discrete lines at 1.14 Hz RBW, no carrier or symbol rate recoverable from x^2 or x^4 (3.2 and 4.8 dB peaks), a unimodal instantaneous-frequency histogram (not FSK) and constant modulus. The one periodicity that looked like a frame rate - 292.969 Hz and at least 27 harmonics, which is 2.4 MHz / 8192 - is a capture-chain artefact: it is present in pure noise and on the receiver's own DC line (see capture_artefact in hackriff:provenance)"
            }
          ],
          "open": "Whether the oscillator radiates into the antenna or couples into the receive chain conducted (host supply, USB) is not decided by these captures: all four were taken with the same antenna and host, and the 20 Msps pair is quantisation-limited, so turning the amp off changed the family's excess over the floor no more than it changed the real stations'. A capture with a 50 ohm terminator in place of the antenna would settle it, and is already on the project's list of things that need the user.",
          "measured_by": "T-317: mean periodograms with a 440 kHz running-median baseline over all four captures (RBW 293-305 Hz), power-weighted centroid and 99 % occupied bandwidth of each above-floor region, plus baseband extraction at 240 and 50 kHz for the modulus, discriminator and nonlinear tests"
        }
